1. Introduction

The Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU) is the computational heart of any digital processor. It performs arithmetic (addition, subtraction), logic (AND, OR, XOR), and shift/compare operations. Designing an ALU from scratch, verifying it, and pushing it through an RTL-to-GDSII flow demonstrates mastery of the entire ASIC design process — from abstract behavior to physical layout.

This project involved:
	•	Designing a 32-bit ALU in Verilog
	•	Functionally verifying it with cocotb (Python) and Verilator
	•	Debugging via GTKWave waveforms
	•	Synthesizing with Yosys into a gate-level netlist
	•	Mapping to Sky130 PDK standard cells
	•	Preparing the design for OpenLane physical flow (PnR → GDSII)

2. Design Objectives
	•	Implement a parameterized ALU supporting arithmetic, logic, shift, and comparison operations.
	•	Verify the ALU using Python-based testbenches (cocotb).
	•	Enable waveform dumping for visual debugging in GTKWave.
	•	Perform RTL-to-gate synthesis using Yosys.
	•	Map to Sky130 standard cell libraries and prepare the design for OpenLane PnR flow.

3. Tools Used
